    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this study we combine outcrop observations and laboratory analyses of tabular zones in sandstone, predominantly compaction localization. Compaction bands are exposed in outcrops of the Jurassic aeolian Aztec Sandstone in the Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada. They are characterized by volume loss, micro fracturing and varying degrees of cataclasis (grain crushing or comminution) and have no or relatively small shear offset across them. Based on geometric and microtextural analyses four types of compaction bands are identified.\r\n\r\na) bed-parallel compaction bands, 0.2-2 cm thick, straight and continuous over lengths of individual dunes (tens of meters),\r\nb) bed-parallel inclined compaction bands, essentially similar to the previous type, except higher dip angles,\r\nc) low angle compaction bands, 0.1-0.5 cm thick, usually confined in between individual bed-parallel compaction bands, with lengths up to several meters,\r\nd) high angle compaction bands, up to 5 cm thick, composed of a zone of closely spaced multiple bands or branching bands over lengths of meters to tens of meters.\r\n\r\nObservations, from high angle bands show that, porosity varies across a zone of compaction bands. Depending on the spacing between each band, the porosity and the associated permeability may change significantly within this zone. The mechanical anisotropy induced by bedding may inhibit propagation or may force single bands to fork (branch). Linear Elastic Fracture Mechanics (LEFM) theory can be used to explain some of the observed propagation paths. Furthermore, a simple Multi-Particle Finite Element Model (MPFEM) is introduced to investigate the stress state within the grains.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
